#: ../../circom.md:0
msgid "Write, compile, and test ZK circuits in Remix IDE using the Circom plugin to generate proofs and verify them on-chain or off-chain with no local setup required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:0
msgid "circom, zero knowledge, zk proof, circom compiler, snarkjs, groth16, r1cs, witness, remix ide, zkp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:8
msgid "Circom Compiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:10
msgid "Circom is a low-level language for writing ZK (zero-knowledge) circuits, programs that express computations as mathematical constraints, making them provable without revealing private inputs. The Remix Circom plugin brings the entire workflow into the browser, with no local setup required. This tutorial walks you through writing a circuit and verifying a proof using the **Hash Checker** template as a worked example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:12
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:14
msgid "Generating a ZK proof in Remix involves three broad stages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:16
msgid "**Build the circuit**: You write your computation in Circom, compile it into a format the browser can execute, and generate the constraint file that defines the rules the computation must follow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:18
msgid "**Execute the circuit**: You provide your private inputs and compute the witness, the full record of how the circuit ran with those inputs. This is what you will eventually prove, without revealing the inputs themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:20
msgid "**Set up keys, generate, and verify the proof**: A one-time trusted setup produces the cryptographic keys tied to your circuit. Those keys, combined with the witness, are used to generate the proof. The proof is then verified either off-chain using a verification key, or on-chain using a generated Solidity contract."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:22
msgid "Each step in this tutorial maps to one of these stages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:24
msgid "Loading a Circom template"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:26
msgid "To load a Circom template, click the dropdown at the center of the Top Bar, and click the revealed \"**Create new Workspace**\" button. On the templates modal under \"Workspace Templates\", scroll till you find \"Circom ZKP\" and select the **Hash Checker** template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:28
msgid "![Circom ZKP template selection in the Remix workspace templates modal](images/circom/circom-template.png)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:28
msgid "Circom ZKP template selection in the Remix workspace templates modal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:30
msgid "This template contains `calculate_hash.circom` and the scripts needed to complete the full proof workflow in this tutorial. After generating the workspace, the Circom plugin will automatically be activated and its icon will appear in the Icon Panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:32
msgid "Step 1: Compile the circuit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:34
msgid "The browser and the snarkjs library that powers this plugin cannot work with `.circom` files directly; they need a binary format they can actually execute. Compilation translates your circuit into a `.wasm` (WebAssembly) file that can be run in the browser to compute values later in the workflow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:36
msgid "To compile:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:38
msgid "Open `circuits/calculate_hash.circom` in the Editor so it is the active file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:39
msgid "Go to the Circom plugin panel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:40
msgid "Click \"Compile `calculate_hash.circom`\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../circom.md:42
msgid "This generates `calculate_hash.wasm` in `circuits/.bin/calculate_hash_js`."
msgstr ""

